T("Chemistry","The mole and chemical amounts","Molar mass and stoichiometry","⚖️","",
 ["A mole is 6.02 × 10²³ particles. The molar mass is the mass of one mole, in grams per mole.", "Moles equal mass divided by molar mass.", "A balanced equation gives the ratio of moles. The limiting reactant runs out first and decides how much product forms."],
 ["About how many particles are in one mole?|6.02 × 10²³|6.02 × 10³|1.00 × 10²³|3.14 × 10²³", "What is the molar mass of water, H₂O? (H = 1, O = 16)|18 g/mol|17 g/mol|10 g/mol|34 g/mol", "What is the molar mass of carbon dioxide, CO₂? (C = 12, O = 16)|44 g/mol|28 g/mol|32 g/mol|40 g/mol", "How many moles are in 36 g of water? (18 g/mol)|2 mol|0.5 mol|18 mol|648 mol", "What is the mass of 3 mol of carbon? (12 g/mol)|36 g|4 g|15 g|0.25 g", "In 2 H₂ + O₂ → 2 H₂O, how many moles of water form from 4 mol of H₂?|4 mol|2 mol|8 mol|1 mol", "What is the reactant that is used up first called?|Limiting reactant|Excess reactant|Catalyst|Product", "A reaction should give 10 g but gives 8 g. What is the percent yield?|80%|125%|8%|20%", "What is the volume of one mole of gas at standard temperature and pressure?|22.4 L|1 L|6.02 L|100 L", "What formula shows the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms?|Empirical formula|Molecular formula|Structural formula|Ionic formula"]);
T("Chemistry","Solutions and concentration","Solute, solvent and moles per litre","💧","",
 ["A solution is a solute dissolved in a solvent.", "Concentration is moles of solute divided by litres of solution, in mol/L.", "When you dilute a solution, the moles of solute stay the same: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂."],
 ["In salt water, what is the salt?|The solute|The solvent|The solution|The precipitate", "In salt water, what is the water?|The solvent|The solute|The mixture|The residue", "2 mol of solute is dissolved to make 4 L of solution. What is the concentration?|0.5 mol/L|2 mol/L|8 mol/L|6 mol/L", "How many moles of solute are in 2 L of a 3 mol/L solution?|6 mol|1.5 mol|5 mol|0.67 mol", "100 mL of a 2 mol/L solution is diluted to 400 mL. What is the new concentration?|0.5 mol/L|8 mol/L|1 mol/L|2 mol/L", "What is a solution that cannot dissolve any more solute called?|Saturated|Dilute|Unsaturated|Neutral", "For most solids, what happens to solubility as temperature rises?|It increases|It decreases|It stays the same|It becomes zero", "For gases, what happens to solubility in water as temperature rises?|It decreases|It increases|It stays the same|It doubles", "Why does water dissolve many ionic compounds?|Water molecules are polar|Water is a metal|Water has no charge at all|Water is an acid", "What is a substance that conducts electricity when dissolved in water called?|Electrolyte|Insulator|Solvent|Polymer"]);
T("Physics","Motion","Velocity, acceleration and graphs","🚗","",
 ["Velocity is displacement divided by time, and it has a direction.", "Acceleration is the change in velocity divided by time.", "On a position-time graph the slope is the velocity. On a velocity-time graph the slope is the acceleration and the area under the line is the displacement."],
 ["A car goes from 0 to 20 m/s in 5 s. What is its acceleration?|4 m/s²|100 m/s²|25 m/s²|0.25 m/s²", "A cyclist rides at 15 m/s for 4 s. How far does she travel?|60 m|3.75 m|19 m|11 m", "What does the slope of a position-time graph give?|Velocity|Acceleration|Distance|Force", "What does the slope of a velocity-time graph give?|Acceleration|Velocity|Displacement|Mass", "What does the area under a velocity-time graph give?|Displacement|Acceleration|Speed|Time", "Which of these is a vector quantity?|Velocity|Speed|Distance|Time", "What is the acceleration due to gravity near Earth's surface?|9.8 m/s²|8.9 m/s²|98 m/s²|1.6 m/s²", "A ball is dropped from rest. How fast is it moving after 2 s? (g = 9.8 m/s²)|19.6 m/s|9.8 m/s|4.9 m/s|39.2 m/s", "An object starts from rest and accelerates at 2 m/s² for 3 s. How far does it go?|9 m|6 m|12 m|18 m", "A runner goes 100 m east, then 40 m west. What is the displacement?|60 m east|140 m east|60 m west|140 m"]);
T("Physics","Forces and Newton's laws","F = m × a","🍎","",
 ["Newton's first law: an object keeps its motion unless a net force acts on it.", "Newton's second law: net force equals mass times acceleration.", "Newton's third law: for every action force there is an equal and opposite reaction force."],
 ["A 10 kg cart accelerates at 3 m/s². What is the net force?|30 N|13 N|3.3 N|7 N", "A 20 N net force acts on a 4 kg object. What is the acceleration?|5 m/s²|80 m/s²|16 m/s²|0.2 m/s²", "What is the weight of a 5 kg object on Earth? (g = 9.8 m/s²)|49 N|5 N|14.8 N|0.51 N", "A box is pushed right with 10 N and left with 4 N. What is the net force?|6 N to the right|14 N to the right|6 N to the left|14 N to the left", "Which law explains why you move forward when a bus stops suddenly?|Newton's first law|Newton's second law|Newton's third law|The law of gravitation", "A swimmer pushes water backward and moves forward. Which law is this?|Newton's third law|Newton's first law|Newton's second law|Ohm's law", "What is the tendency of an object to resist changes in motion called?|Inertia|Friction|Momentum|Weight", "What is the support force a surface pushes up with called?|Normal force|Friction|Tension|Gravity", "If the net force on an object is zero, what is true?|Its velocity is constant|It must be at rest|It speeds up|It slows down", "What is the unit of mass?|Kilogram|Newton|Joule|Metre per second"]);
T("Physics","Work, energy and power","Joules and watts","🔋","",
 ["Work is force times distance and is measured in joules.", "Kinetic energy is one half times mass times speed squared. Gravitational potential energy is mass times g times height.", "Power is work divided by time and is measured in watts. Energy is never created or destroyed. It only changes form."],
 ["A 50 N force moves a box 4 m. How much work is done?|200 J|12.5 J|54 J|46 J", "What is the kinetic energy of a 2 kg ball moving at 3 m/s?|9 J|6 J|18 J|3 J", "What is the potential energy of a 2 kg book 5 m above the floor? (g = 9.8 m/s²)|98 J|10 J|49 J|16.8 J", "A motor does 600 J of work in 3 s. What is its power?|200 W|1800 W|603 W|0.005 W", "What is the unit of energy?|Joule|Watt|Newton|Volt", "What is the unit of power?|Watt|Joule|Newton|Ampere", "A machine takes in 120 J and gives 30 J of useful energy. What is its efficiency?|25%|40%|75%|90%", "What does the law of conservation of energy say?|Energy cannot be created or destroyed|Energy is always lost|Energy is created by motion|Energy only exists as heat", "As a ball falls, what happens to its energy?|Potential energy changes to kinetic energy|Kinetic energy changes to potential energy|All of its energy disappears|Its energy doubles", "If speed doubles, what happens to kinetic energy?|It becomes four times as large|It doubles|It stays the same|It halves"]);
T("Biology","Meiosis and inheritance","How sex cells are made","🧬","",
 ["Meiosis makes sex cells, called gametes, with half the usual number of chromosomes.", "Human body cells have 46 chromosomes. Gametes have 23.", "Crossing over and the random sorting of chromosomes make each gamete different."],
 ["What does meiosis produce?|Gametes with half the number of chromosomes|Two identical body cells|Skin cells|Cells with double the chromosomes", "How many chromosomes does a human body cell have?|46|23|92|48", "How many chromosomes does a human gamete have?|23|46|22|92", "How many cells result from one cell going through meiosis?|Four|Two|One|Eight", "What is a cell with two sets of chromosomes called?|Diploid|Haploid|Triploid|Gamete", "What is a cell with one set of chromosomes called?|Haploid|Diploid|Zygote|Somatic", "What is crossing over?|The exchange of segments between paired chromosomes|The splitting of the cell|The copying of DNA|The joining of gametes", "What forms when a sperm and an egg join?|A zygote|A gamete|A haploid cell|A spore", "Which sex chromosomes does a typical human male have?|XY|XX|YY|XO", "How many divisions happen in meiosis?|Two|One|Three|Four", "Why is meiosis important for variation?|It mixes genes into new combinations|It makes identical copies|It removes all mutations|It doubles the chromosomes"]);
T("Chemistry","Bonding and periodic trends","Ionic, covalent and patterns in the table","⚛️","",
 ["Ionic bonds form when a metal gives electrons to a non-metal. Covalent bonds form when non-metals share electrons.", "Atomic radius gets smaller across a period and larger down a group.", "Electronegativity gets larger across a period and smaller down a group."],
 ["What kind of bond forms between a metal and a non-metal?|Ionic|Covalent|Metallic|Hydrogen", "What kind of bond forms when two non-metals share electrons?|Covalent|Ionic|Metallic|Nuclear", "What happens to atomic radius across a period, left to right?|It decreases|It increases|It stays the same|It doubles", "What happens to atomic radius down a group?|It increases|It decreases|It stays the same|It halves", "Which element is the most electronegative?|Fluorine|Francium|Helium|Sodium", "What charge does a sodium ion have?|1+|1−|2+|0", "What charge does a chloride ion have?|1−|1+|2−|0", "How many valence electrons do the noble gases other than helium have?|8|2|1|7", "What is electronegativity?|An atom's ability to attract shared electrons|The mass of an atom|The number of neutrons|The size of an atom", "What kind of bond is in a water molecule?|Polar covalent|Ionic|Non-polar covalent|Metallic", "Which group contains the alkali metals?|Group 1|Group 2|Group 17|Group 18"]);
T("Chemistry","Gases and the gas laws","Pressure, volume and temperature","🎈","",
 ["Boyle's law: at constant temperature, pressure and volume are inversely related. P1V1 = P2V2.", "Charles's law: at constant pressure, volume is directly related to temperature in kelvins.", "To change degrees Celsius to kelvins, add 273."],
 ["What happens to the volume of a gas when pressure doubles at constant temperature?|It halves|It doubles|It stays the same|It quadruples", "Which law relates pressure and volume?|Boyle's law|Charles's law|Avogadro's law|Ohm's law", "Which law relates volume and temperature?|Charles's law|Boyle's law|Newton's law|Hooke's law", "What is 27 degrees Celsius in kelvins?|300 K|246 K|27 K|327 K", "What is 0 K called?|Absolute zero|Freezing point|Boiling point|Room temperature", "A gas has a volume of 4 L at 100 kPa. What is its volume at 200 kPa, at the same temperature?|2 L|8 L|4 L|1 L", "A gas has a volume of 2 L at 300 K. What is its volume at 600 K, at the same pressure?|4 L|1 L|2 L|6 L", "What is standard atmospheric pressure?|101.3 kPa|1 kPa|273 kPa|22.4 kPa", "What causes gas pressure?|Particles colliding with the walls of the container|Particles sticking together|Gravity alone|The colour of the gas", "What happens to gas particles when temperature rises?|They move faster|They move slower|They stop moving|They get heavier", "What is the ideal gas law?|PV = nRT|P = VI|F = ma|E = mc²"]);
T("Physics","Waves and sound","Frequency, wavelength and speed","🔊","",
 ["A wave carries energy without carrying matter. The wave equation is v = f × λ: speed = frequency × wavelength.", "Frequency is measured in hertz. The period is the time for one wave, T = 1 ÷ f.", "Sound is a longitudinal wave that needs a medium. It travels at about 343 m/s in air at 20 °C."],
 ["What is the wave equation?|v = f × λ|v = f ÷ λ|v = λ ÷ f|v = f + λ", "What unit is frequency measured in?|Hertz|Metres|Seconds|Newtons", "A wave has a frequency of 10 Hz and a wavelength of 2 m. What is its speed?|20 m/s|5 m/s|12 m/s|8 m/s", "A wave has a frequency of 5 Hz. What is its period?|0.2 s|5 s|2 s|0.5 s", "What kind of wave is sound?|Longitudinal|Transverse|Electromagnetic|Standing only", "About how fast does sound travel in air at 20 °C?|343 m/s|3 m/s|3,000 m/s|300,000 km/s", "Can sound travel through a vacuum?|No|Yes|Only loud sounds|Only at night", "What does the amplitude of a sound wave affect?|Loudness|Pitch|Speed|Wavelength only", "What does the frequency of a sound wave affect?|Pitch|Loudness|Speed in air|Amplitude", "What is the Doppler effect?|A change in observed frequency when the source or observer moves|An echo|A louder sound|A wave stopping", "What happens when two waves meet crest to crest?|Constructive interference|Destructive interference|Reflection|Refraction"]);
T("Physics","Electricity and magnetism","Circuits, fields and motors","🧲","",
 ["Ohm's law: V = I × R. In a series circuit the current is the same everywhere. In a parallel circuit the voltage is the same across each branch.", "A current in a wire creates a magnetic field around it.", "A motor turns electrical energy into motion. A generator turns motion into electrical energy."],
 ["What is Ohm's law?|V = I × R|V = I ÷ R|V = R ÷ I|V = I + R", "A 12 V battery is connected to a 4 Ω resistor. What is the current?|3 A|48 A|8 A|16 A", "Two resistors of 3 Ω and 6 Ω are in series. What is the total resistance?|9 Ω|2 Ω|18 Ω|3 Ω", "Two resistors of 6 Ω and 6 Ω are in parallel. What is the total resistance?|3 Ω|12 Ω|6 Ω|36 Ω", "In a series circuit, what is the same at every point?|The current|The voltage|The resistance|The power", "In a parallel circuit, what is the same across every branch?|The voltage|The current|The resistance|The charge", "What surrounds a wire that carries a current?|A magnetic field|A vacuum|A sound wave|Nothing", "What does an electric motor do?|Changes electrical energy into motion|Changes motion into electrical energy|Stores charge|Measures current", "What does a generator do?|Changes motion into electrical energy|Changes electrical energy into motion|Stores energy as heat|Blocks current", "What does a transformer change?|Voltage|Frequency|Resistance of a wire|The direction of current only", "What is produced when a magnet moves through a coil of wire?|An electric current|A sound|Light only|Static charge only"]);
T("Biology","Plants: structure and growth","Roots, stems, leaves and hormones","🌱","",
 ["Xylem carries water and minerals up from the roots. Phloem carries sugars made in the leaves to the rest of the plant.", "Gases enter and leave a leaf through tiny openings called stomata. Guard cells open and close them.", "Plants grow toward light. This is called phototropism, and it is controlled by plant hormones called auxins."],
 ["Which tissue carries water up from the roots?|Xylem|Phloem|Epidermis|Cambium", "Which tissue carries sugars around the plant?|Phloem|Xylem|Cuticle|Root hair", "What are the tiny openings in a leaf called?|Stomata|Chloroplasts|Vacuoles|Petals", "Which cells open and close the stomata?|Guard cells|Root cells|Xylem cells|Pollen cells", "What is the loss of water vapour from leaves called?|Transpiration|Respiration|Germination|Pollination", "What is growth toward light called?|Phototropism|Gravitropism|Photosynthesis|Transpiration", "Which plant hormones control growth toward light?|Auxins|Insulin|Adrenaline|Enzymes", "What is the main job of root hairs?|To take in water and minerals|To make sugar|To make seeds|To catch sunlight", "In which part of a flower is pollen made?|The anther|The ovary|The sepal|The root", "What is the growth of a root downward in response to gravity called?|Gravitropism|Phototropism|Transpiration|Fertilization"]);
T("Chemistry","Limiting reactant and percent yield","How much product you really get","🧮","",
 ["The limiting reactant is used up first, so it decides how much product can form. The other reactant is in excess.", "The theoretical yield is the amount of product predicted by the balanced equation. The actual yield is what is measured in the lab.", "Percent yield = actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100."],
 ["What is the limiting reactant?|The reactant that is used up first|The reactant that is left over|The product with the most mass|The catalyst", "What is the reactant that is left over called?|The excess reactant|The limiting reactant|The product|The solvent", "What is the formula for percent yield?|Actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100|Theoretical yield ÷ actual yield × 100|Actual yield × theoretical yield|Actual yield − theoretical yield", "The theoretical yield is 20 g and the actual yield is 15 g. What is the percent yield?|75%|133%|5%|35%", "The theoretical yield is 50 g and the percent yield is 80%. What is the actual yield?|40 g|62.5 g|30 g|130 g", "In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, 4 mol of H₂ react with 1 mol of O₂. Which is the limiting reactant?|O₂|H₂|H₂O|Neither", "In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, how many moles of water form from 1 mol of O₂ with plenty of H₂?|2 mol|1 mol|4 mol|0.5 mol", "In N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, how many moles of H₂ are needed to react with 2 mol of N₂?|6 mol|3 mol|2 mol|1.5 mol", "Which is a reason the actual yield is often lower than the theoretical yield?|Some product is lost when it is collected|Atoms are destroyed|The balanced equation is always wrong|Mass is created", "Which yield is measured in the lab?|Actual yield|Theoretical yield|Percent yield|Molar yield"]);
T("Physics","Heat and specific heat capacity","Warming things up","🌡","",
 ["Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of the particles. Heat is energy that moves from a warmer object to a cooler one.", "Specific heat capacity is the energy needed to warm 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C. For water it is about 4200 J/(kg·°C).", "Q = m × c × ΔT, where Q is the heat, m is the mass, c is the specific heat capacity and ΔT is the change in temperature."],
 ["What does temperature measure?|The average kinetic energy of the particles|The total mass of the particles|The number of particles|The colour of an object", "In which direction does heat move?|From warmer to cooler objects|From cooler to warmer objects|Only upward|Only through metals", "What is the formula for heat gained or lost?|Q = m × c × ΔT|Q = m ÷ c|Q = m × g × h|Q = V × I", "What is the specific heat capacity of water, about?|4200 J/(kg·°C)|42 J/(kg·°C)|420 000 J/(kg·°C)|1 J/(kg·°C)", "How much heat is needed to warm 2 kg of water by 10 °C? Use c = 4200 J/(kg·°C).|84 000 J|8400 J|42 000 J|840 J", "How much heat is needed to warm 1 kg of water by 1 °C?|4200 J|1 J|420 J|100 J", "What is the unit of heat?|Joule|Watt|Newton|Kelvin", "Water has a high specific heat capacity. What does that mean?|It warms up and cools down slowly|It warms up very quickly|It cannot store heat|It boils at a low temperature", "What happens to the temperature of ice while it is melting?|It stays the same|It rises quickly|It falls|It doubles", "What is 0 °C in kelvin, about?|273 K|0 K|100 K|373 K"]);

