T("Biology","Biochemistry","Molecules of life and enzymes","🥚","",
 ["The four large molecules of life are carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids.", "Proteins are chains of amino acids. Enzymes are proteins that speed up reactions in the body.", "An enzyme fits its substrate at the active site. Heat or a change in pH can change its shape so it stops working."],
 ["What are the building blocks of proteins?|Amino acids|Fatty acids|Nucleotides|Simple sugars", "What are the building blocks of nucleic acids such as DNA?|Nucleotides|Amino acids|Glycerol|Glucose", "What does an enzyme do?|Speeds up a chemical reaction|Stores genetic information|Carries oxygen|Makes the cell wall", "What is the part of an enzyme where the substrate binds called?|Active site|Nucleus|Peptide bond|Cell membrane", "What is it called when heat changes the shape of a protein so it stops working?|Denaturation|Digestion|Replication|Condensation", "What is the chemical formula of glucose?|C₆H₁₂O₆|CO₂|H₂O|C₂H₅OH", "Which molecule is the main energy currency of the cell?|ATP|DNA|Glucose|Starch", "Where does most of cellular respiration take place?|Mitochondria|Ribosomes|Nucleus|Golgi body", "Where does glycolysis take place?|Cytoplasm|Mitochondria|Nucleus|Chloroplast", "Where does photosynthesis take place?|Chloroplasts|Mitochondria|Ribosomes|Vacuoles"]);
T("Biology","DNA and making proteins","Replication, transcription and translation","🧬","",
 ["DNA is a double helix. Base A pairs with T, and C pairs with G.", "In transcription, a gene in the DNA is copied into messenger RNA in the nucleus.", "In translation, a ribosome reads the messenger RNA three bases at a time and joins amino acids into a protein."],
 ["In DNA, which base pairs with adenine (A)?|Thymine|Guanine|Cytosine|Uracil", "In DNA, which base pairs with cytosine (C)?|Guanine|Adenine|Thymine|Uracil", "Which base is found in RNA but not in DNA?|Uracil|Thymine|Adenine|Guanine", "What is the process of copying DNA into messenger RNA called?|Transcription|Translation|Replication|Mutation", "What is the process of building a protein from messenger RNA called?|Translation|Transcription|Replication|Respiration", "Where does translation take place?|At a ribosome|In the nucleus|In a vacuole|On the cell wall", "How many bases make up one codon?|Three|One|Two|Four", "Which molecule carries amino acids to the ribosome?|Transfer RNA|Messenger RNA|DNA polymerase|ATP", "Each new DNA molecule has one old strand and one new strand. What is this called?|Semi-conservative replication|Conservative replication|Transcription|Translation", "What is a change in the base sequence of DNA called?|Mutation|Translation|Codon|Enzyme"]);
T("Biology","Homeostasis","Keeping the body in balance","🌡","",
 ["Homeostasis is keeping conditions inside the body steady, such as temperature and blood sugar.", "Most control uses negative feedback: a change is detected and the body acts to reverse it.", "The nervous system sends fast electrical signals. The endocrine system sends slower chemical signals called hormones."],
 ["What is homeostasis?|Keeping internal conditions steady|Growing new cells|Breaking down food|Copying DNA", "What kind of feedback reverses a change to bring the body back to normal?|Negative feedback|Positive feedback|Neutral feedback|Forward feedback", "Which hormone lowers blood sugar?|Insulin|Glucagon|Adrenaline|Thyroxine", "Which hormone raises blood sugar?|Glucagon|Insulin|Estrogen|ADH", "Which organ makes insulin?|Pancreas|Liver|Kidney|Stomach", "Which part of the brain controls body temperature?|Hypothalamus|Cerebellum|Cerebrum|Spinal cord", "What is the gap between two nerve cells called?|Synapse|Axon|Dendrite|Myelin", "What is the filtering unit of the kidney called?|Nephron|Neuron|Alveolus|Villus", "Which hormone tells the kidneys to save water?|ADH|Insulin|Glucagon|Adrenaline", "Which hormone prepares the body for fight or flight?|Adrenaline|Insulin|ADH|Melatonin"]);
T("Chemistry","Organic chemistry","Carbon compounds and functional groups","⛽","",
 ["Organic chemistry is the study of carbon compounds. A carbon atom forms four bonds.", "Hydrocarbons contain only carbon and hydrogen. Alkanes have single bonds, alkenes have a double bond and alkynes have a triple bond.", "A functional group gives a molecule its properties. Alcohols have an OH group and carboxylic acids have a COOH group."],
 ["How many bonds does a carbon atom usually form?|Four|Two|Three|One", "What is the formula of methane?|CH₄|C₂H₆|CO₂|CH₃OH", "What is a compound made of only carbon and hydrogen called?|Hydrocarbon|Carbohydrate|Alcohol|Ester", "Which family of hydrocarbons has only single bonds?|Alkanes|Alkenes|Alkynes|Aromatics", "Which family of hydrocarbons has a carbon to carbon double bond?|Alkenes|Alkanes|Alkynes|Alcohols", "How many carbon atoms are in propane?|Three|One|Two|Four", "What is the general formula of the alkanes?|CₙH₂ₙ₊₂|CₙH₂ₙ|CₙH₂ₙ₋₂|CₙHₙ", "Which functional group is found in alcohols?|Hydroxyl, OH|Carboxyl, COOH|Amino, NH₂|Carbonyl, CO", "What are molecules with the same formula but different structures called?|Isomers|Isotopes|Ions|Polymers", "What is a very long molecule made of many repeating units called?|Polymer|Monomer|Isomer|Alkane"]);
T("Chemistry","Rates, equilibrium and acids","How fast and how far reactions go","⏱","",
 ["A reaction goes faster with higher temperature, higher concentration, more surface area or a catalyst.", "At equilibrium the forward and reverse reactions happen at the same rate, so the amounts stop changing.", "Le Chatelier's principle: if a system at equilibrium is disturbed, it shifts to reduce the change. pH is the negative log of the hydrogen ion concentration."],
 ["Which change makes a reaction go faster?|Raising the temperature|Lowering the concentration|Using bigger lumps|Cooling the mixture", "What is the minimum energy needed for a reaction to happen called?|Activation energy|Bond energy|Kinetic energy|Enthalpy", "How does a catalyst speed up a reaction?|It lowers the activation energy|It raises the temperature|It adds more reactant|It increases the pressure", "What is true at chemical equilibrium?|Forward and reverse rates are equal|The reaction has stopped|All reactants are used up|Only products remain", "More reactant is added to a system at equilibrium. Which way does it shift?|Toward the products|Toward the reactants|It does not shift|It stops", "A reaction releases heat. What is it called?|Exothermic|Endothermic|Isothermal|Neutral", "What is the sign of the enthalpy change for an exothermic reaction?|Negative|Positive|Zero|It has no sign", "The hydrogen ion concentration is 1 × 10⁻³ mol/L. What is the pH?|3|11|−3|0.001", "Which of these is a strong acid?|Hydrochloric acid|Acetic acid|Carbonic acid|Citric acid", "What is a solution that resists changes in pH called?|Buffer|Indicator|Catalyst|Electrolyte"]);
T("Physics","Momentum, circular motion and fields","Collisions, orbits and forces at a distance","🛰","",
 ["Momentum is mass times velocity. The total momentum of a closed system stays the same in a collision.", "An object moving in a circle accelerates toward the centre. This centripetal acceleration is speed squared divided by radius.", "Gravity and the electric force both get weaker with the square of the distance."],
 ["What is the momentum of a 2 kg ball moving at 5 m/s?|10 kg·m/s|2.5 kg·m/s|7 kg·m/s|50 kg·m/s", "What is force multiplied by the time it acts called?|Impulse|Work|Power|Torque", "What stays the same in every collision in a closed system?|Total momentum|Total kinetic energy|Each object's speed|Each object's direction", "In which type of collision is kinetic energy also conserved?|Elastic|Inelastic|Completely inelastic|Explosive", "A car moves at 10 m/s around a curve of radius 20 m. What is its centripetal acceleration?|5 m/s²|2 m/s²|200 m/s²|0.5 m/s²", "In which direction does centripetal force act?|Toward the centre of the circle|Away from the centre|Along the direction of motion|Straight up", "The distance between two masses doubles. What happens to the gravitational force?|It becomes one quarter|It becomes one half|It doubles|It stays the same", "For a projectile with no air resistance, what is true of its horizontal velocity?|It stays constant|It increases|It decreases|It becomes zero at the top", "Which law gives the force between two electric charges?|Coulomb's law|Ohm's law|Hooke's law|Boyle's law", "What is the unit of electric field strength?|Newton per coulomb|Joule|Ampere|Ohm"]);
T("Physics","Waves and modern physics","Light as a wave and as a particle","🌈","",
 ["For any wave, speed equals frequency times wavelength.", "Interference and diffraction show that light behaves as a wave. The photoelectric effect shows that light also comes in packets called photons.", "The energy of a photon is Planck's constant times its frequency. Einstein showed that mass and energy are linked by E = mc²."],
 ["A wave has a frequency of 5 Hz and a wavelength of 2 m. What is its speed?|10 m/s|2.5 m/s|7 m/s|0.4 m/s", "What is the unit of frequency?|Hertz|Metre|Second|Joule", "What is the bending of a wave around an obstacle or through a gap called?|Diffraction|Reflection|Refraction|Absorption", "What happens when two waves meet and combine?|Interference|Diffraction|Polarization|Dispersion", "Which experiment showed that light acts as a wave?|Young's double-slit experiment|The photoelectric effect|Millikan's oil drop experiment|The gold foil experiment", "Which effect showed that light acts as a particle?|The photoelectric effect|Diffraction|Interference|Refraction", "What is a packet of light energy called?|Photon|Proton|Electron|Neutron", "If the frequency of light increases, what happens to the energy of each photon?|It increases|It decreases|It stays the same|It becomes zero", "Which equation links mass and energy?|E = mc²|F = ma|V = IR|v = fλ", "According to special relativity, what is the same for all observers?|The speed of light in a vacuum|The length of an object|The passing of time|The mass of an object"]);
T("Biology","Cellular respiration and photosynthesis","How cells get and store energy","🔋","",
 ["Cellular respiration releases energy from glucose and stores it as ATP: glucose + oxygen gives carbon dioxide + water + ATP.", "It has three main stages: glycolysis, the Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain.", "Photosynthesis is the reverse overall. It stores light energy in glucose."],
 ["What molecule do cells use directly for energy?|ATP|DNA|Glucose only|Starch", "Where does glycolysis take place?|In the cytoplasm|In the nucleus|In the chloroplast|In the ribosome", "Where does the Krebs cycle take place?|In the mitochondria|In the cytoplasm|In the nucleus|In the cell wall", "Which stage of aerobic respiration makes the most ATP?|The electron transport chain|Glycolysis|The Krebs cycle|Fermentation", "What is the final electron acceptor in aerobic respiration?|Oxygen|Carbon dioxide|Glucose|Water", "What does glycolysis split glucose into?|Two pyruvate molecules|Two ATP only|Carbon dioxide|Oxygen", "What is made when muscle cells ferment without oxygen?|Lactic acid|Ethanol|Glucose|Oxygen", "What does yeast make in fermentation?|Ethanol and carbon dioxide|Lactic acid|Oxygen|Glucose", "Where do the light reactions of photosynthesis take place?|In the thylakoid membranes|In the stroma|In the mitochondria|In the nucleus", "What does the Calvin cycle produce?|Sugar|Oxygen|Water|Light", "Which gas does photosynthesis release?|Oxygen|Carbon dioxide|Nitrogen|Methane"]);
T("Biology","Population dynamics","How populations grow and are limited","📈","",
 ["A population grows when births and immigration are greater than deaths and emigration.", "Carrying capacity is the largest population an environment can support over time.", "Exponential growth gives a J-shaped curve. Logistic growth levels off in an S-shaped curve."],
 ["What is carrying capacity?|The largest population an environment can support over time|The number of births in a year|The size of a habitat|The weight an animal can carry", "What shape is an exponential growth curve?|J-shaped|S-shaped|Flat|U-shaped", "What shape is a logistic growth curve?|S-shaped|J-shaped|Straight line|V-shaped", "Which factor increases a population?|Immigration|Emigration|Death|Predation", "Which is a density-dependent limiting factor?|Competition for food|A forest fire|A flood|A cold snap", "Which is a density-independent limiting factor?|A drought|Disease|Competition|Predation", "What is population density?|The number of individuals in a given area|The total number of species|The birth rate|The age of a population", "What is a relationship called in which both species benefit?|Mutualism|Parasitism|Predation|Competition", "What is a relationship called in which one species benefits and the other is harmed?|Parasitism|Mutualism|Commensalism|Neutralism", "What does an age pyramid with a wide base show?|A growing population|A shrinking population|A stable population|An extinct population", "What happens when a population overshoots its carrying capacity?|It usually declines|It grows forever|Nothing changes|The habitat grows"]);
T("Chemistry","Electrochemistry","Oxidation, reduction and cells","🔌","",
 ["Oxidation is the loss of electrons. Reduction is the gain of electrons. They always happen together.", "In a galvanic cell, a chemical reaction produces electricity. Oxidation happens at the anode and reduction at the cathode.", "An electrolytic cell uses electricity to force a reaction."],
 ["What is oxidation?|The loss of electrons|The gain of electrons|The gain of protons|The loss of neutrons", "What is reduction?|The gain of electrons|The loss of electrons|The loss of protons|The gain of neutrons", "What is a reaction called in which both oxidation and reduction happen?|A redox reaction|A neutralization|A decomposition|A precipitation", "At which electrode does oxidation take place?|The anode|The cathode|Both|Neither", "At which electrode does reduction take place?|The cathode|The anode|The salt bridge|The wire", "What does a galvanic cell do?|Turns chemical energy into electrical energy|Uses electricity to force a reaction|Stores heat|Measures pH", "What does an electrolytic cell do?|Uses electrical energy to drive a reaction|Makes electricity from a reaction|Cools a solution|Filters a liquid", "What is the job of a salt bridge?|To let ions flow and keep the charges balanced|To carry electrons|To heat the cell|To stop the reaction", "In which direction do electrons flow in a galvanic cell?|From the anode to the cathode|From the cathode to the anode|Through the salt bridge|They do not move", "What is the oxidation number of an element in its pure form?|0|1+|1−|2+", "What is corrosion of iron an example of?|Oxidation|Reduction of iron|Neutralization|Sublimation"]);
T("Chemistry","Energy changes in reactions","Exothermic, endothermic and enthalpy","🔥","",
 ["An exothermic reaction releases heat, so its enthalpy change is negative. An endothermic reaction absorbs heat, so its enthalpy change is positive.", "Heat gained or lost is q = m × c × ΔT.", "The specific heat capacity of water is 4.18 J/(g·°C)."],
 ["What does an exothermic reaction do?|Releases heat|Absorbs heat|Makes no energy change|Removes mass", "What does an endothermic reaction do?|Absorbs heat|Releases heat|Releases light only|Creates mass", "What is the sign of the enthalpy change in an exothermic reaction?|Negative|Positive|Zero|It has no sign", "Which is an exothermic process?|Burning wood|Melting ice|Boiling water|Photosynthesis", "Which is an endothermic process?|Melting ice|Burning gas|Freezing water|Rusting", "What is the formula for heat gained or lost?|q = m × c × ΔT|q = m ÷ c|q = c ÷ ΔT|q = m + c + ΔT", "What is the specific heat capacity of water?|4.18 J/(g·°C)|1.00 J/(g·°C)|9.81 J/(g·°C)|0.42 J/(g·°C)", "How much heat warms 10 g of water by 10 °C?|418 J|41.8 J|4.18 J|4,180 J", "What is activation energy?|The energy needed to start a reaction|The energy released at the end|The total energy of the products|The heat of the surroundings", "What does a catalyst do to activation energy?|Lowers it|Raises it|Removes the reaction|Has no effect", "What does Hess's law say?|The total enthalpy change is the same whatever route is taken|Energy is always lost|Heat flows from cold to hot|Mass is not conserved"]);
T("Physics","Projectile motion","Motion in two dimensions","🏀","",
 ["A projectile moves under gravity alone. Its horizontal velocity stays constant, and its vertical velocity changes by 9.8 m/s every second.", "Horizontal and vertical motions are independent.", "Ignoring air resistance, a launch angle of 45 degrees gives the greatest range on level ground."],
 ["What force acts on a projectile, ignoring air resistance?|Gravity only|Thrust only|Friction only|No force", "What happens to the horizontal velocity of a projectile?|It stays constant|It increases|It decreases|It becomes zero", "What is the acceleration due to gravity near Earth's surface?|9.8 m/s²|1.6 m/s²|3.0 m/s²|98 m/s²", "What shape is the path of a projectile?|A parabola|A circle|A straight line|A spiral", "Which launch angle gives the greatest range on level ground, ignoring air resistance?|45 degrees|30 degrees|60 degrees|90 degrees", "What is the vertical velocity of a projectile at the top of its path?|Zero|Its maximum|9.8 m/s|Equal to its horizontal velocity", "A ball is dropped and another is thrown horizontally from the same height. Which lands first?|They land at the same time|The dropped ball|The thrown ball|It depends on their mass", "A ball rolls off a table at 3 m/s and lands 0.5 s later. How far from the table does it land?|1.5 m|6 m|3.5 m|0.17 m", "An object falls from rest for 2 s. About how fast is it moving?|19.6 m/s|9.8 m/s|4.9 m/s|39.2 m/s", "An object falls from rest for 2 s. About how far does it fall?|19.6 m|9.8 m|4.9 m|39.2 m", "Which two launch angles give the same range?|30 and 60 degrees|30 and 45 degrees|45 and 90 degrees|10 and 20 degrees"]);
T("Biology","The nervous and endocrine systems","Nerves and hormones","🧠","",
 ["A neuron is a nerve cell. A nerve signal travels along the axon as an electrical impulse and crosses the gap, called a synapse, using chemicals called neurotransmitters.", "The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord. A reflex is a fast, automatic response.", "The endocrine system uses hormones carried in the blood. Insulin, made in the pancreas, lowers blood sugar."],
 ["What is a neuron?|A nerve cell|A blood cell|A hormone|A muscle fibre", "What is the gap between two neurons called?|Synapse|Axon|Dendrite|Myelin", "What chemicals carry a signal across a synapse?|Neurotransmitters|Enzymes|Antibodies|Hormones in the blood", "Which parts make up the central nervous system?|The brain and spinal cord|The heart and lungs|The nerves in the arms and legs|The glands", "Which part of a neuron carries the impulse away from the cell body?|Axon|Dendrite|Nucleus|Synapse", "What does the myelin sheath do?|Speeds up nerve impulses|Makes hormones|Stores sugar|Stops all signals", "How do hormones travel around the body?|In the blood|Along nerves|Through the air|Inside bones", "Which organ makes insulin?|Pancreas|Liver|Kidney|Thyroid", "What does insulin do?|Lowers blood sugar|Raises blood sugar|Raises heart rate|Digests protein", "Which hormone is released in the \"fight or flight\" response?|Adrenaline|Insulin|Melatonin|Growth hormone", "Which is faster, a nerve signal or a hormone signal?|A nerve signal|A hormone signal|They are the same|Neither moves"]);
T("Chemistry","Atomic structure and molecular shape","Orbitals, VSEPR and forces between molecules","⚛️","",
 ["Electrons occupy orbitals in energy levels. An s sublevel holds up to 2 electrons, a p sublevel up to 6 and a d sublevel up to 10.", "VSEPR theory says electron pairs around a central atom repel each other, and this sets the shape of a molecule. Methane is tetrahedral and water is bent.", "Forces between molecules include London dispersion forces, dipole-dipole forces and hydrogen bonds. Stronger forces mean a higher boiling point."],
 ["How many electrons can an s sublevel hold?|2|6|10|14", "How many electrons can a p sublevel hold?|6|2|8|10", "How many electrons can a d sublevel hold?|10|6|2|14", "What is the electron configuration of carbon, which has 6 electrons?|1s² 2s² 2p²|1s² 2s⁴|1s² 2p⁴|1s⁶", "What is the shape of a methane molecule, CH₄?|Tetrahedral|Linear|Bent|Trigonal planar", "What is the shape of a water molecule?|Bent|Linear|Tetrahedral|Trigonal planar", "What is the shape of a carbon dioxide molecule?|Linear|Bent|Tetrahedral|Trigonal pyramidal", "What is the shape of an ammonia molecule, NH₃?|Trigonal pyramidal|Linear|Tetrahedral|Bent", "What does VSEPR theory say about electron pairs?|They repel each other|They attract each other|They do not affect shape|They leave the atom", "Which force between molecules gives water its high boiling point?|Hydrogen bonding|London dispersion forces only|Ionic bonding|Gravity", "Which force exists between all molecules?|London dispersion forces|Hydrogen bonds|Ionic bonds|Metallic bonds"]);
T("Physics","Relativity and nuclear energy","Very fast and very small","☢️","",
 ["Special relativity says the speed of light in a vacuum is the same for all observers. A clock moving very fast runs slow compared with one at rest. This is called time dilation.", "Mass and energy are linked by E = mc².", "In fission a large nucleus splits. In fusion small nuclei join. Both release energy. A half-life is the time for half of a radioactive sample to decay."],
 ["What is the same for all observers, according to special relativity?|The speed of light in a vacuum|The speed of sound|The length of every object|The time on every clock", "What is it called when a fast-moving clock runs slow?|Time dilation|Length contraction|Refraction|Fission", "What does E = mc² say?|Mass and energy are equivalent|Force equals mass times acceleration|Energy is always lost|Light has no speed", "In E = mc², what does c stand for?|The speed of light|The charge of an electron|The specific heat capacity|A constant equal to 1", "What is nuclear fission?|A large nucleus splits into smaller ones|Small nuclei join together|An electron leaves an atom|A chemical bond breaks", "What is nuclear fusion?|Small nuclei join to make a larger one|A large nucleus splits|An atom gains an electron|A molecule burns", "Which process powers the Sun?|Fusion|Fission|Combustion|Radioactive decay of uranium", "Which process is used in nuclear power stations today?|Fission|Fusion|Combustion|Photosynthesis", "A sample has a half-life of 10 years. How much of 80 g is left after 20 years?|20 g|40 g|10 g|0 g", "A sample has a half-life of 5 days. How much of 100 g is left after 5 days?|50 g|25 g|100 g|0 g", "What is an alpha particle?|A helium nucleus|An electron|A neutron|A light wave", "Which type of radiation is the most penetrating?|Gamma rays|Alpha particles|Beta particles|Visible light"]);

